# Lets the API start and stop the model loop from the admin web page, and
# nothing else.
#
# Install to /etc/sudoers.d/sllm-admin with mode 0440, via visudo -c:
#     sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root \
#         deploy/sllm-admin.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/sllm-admin
#     sudo visudo -c
#
# This does NOT change any human's sudo access. chootka keeps full sudo with a
# password, exactly as before. This grants passwordless sudo to the `sllm`
# service account only, because a web request cannot type a password.
#
# The two commands are fully-qualified and fully-specified: absolute path to
# systemctl, a fixed verb, and a fixed unit name. There is no wildcard. sudo
# matches the whole argv, so `systemctl start something-else`, `systemctl
# daemon-reload`, and `systemctl start sllm-loop.service --now-do-something`
# are all refused. api/admin.py additionally whitelists the verb before calling
# out, so an injection would have to defeat both.
#
# Worth stating plainly: the worst an attacker who fully owns the API process
# can do with this rule is start and stop the model loop -- which is exactly
# what the admin page is for. There is no path from here to a root shell.

sllm ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start sllm-loop.service
sllm ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop sllm-loop.service
